Yes, here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Filter out stop words (e.g., "a", "an", "the")
  const filteredWords = words.filter((word) => !["a", "an", "the"].includes(word));

  // Reverse the filtered array
  const reversedWords = filteredWords.reverse();

  // Concatenate the reversed and original arrays (excluding stop words)
  const result = reversedWords.concat(words.filter((word) => ["a", "an", "the"].includes(word))).join(" ");

  return result;
}

// Example usage
const input = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const output = switchAdjectives(input);
console.log(output);
```

The `switchAdjectives()` function takes a string as its argument, splits it into an array of words, filters out stop words, reverses the filtered array, concatenates it with the original array (excluding stop words), and joins the resulting array back into a string. This effectively switches the positions of adjectives in the sentence. Note that this implementation assumes that adjectives come before nouns in a sentence. If your input sentences have a different structure, you may need to modify the function accordingly.

