To blend multiple audio files using Java, you can use a library called SoX (Simple Operations on Audio Files). Here's an overview of how you could approach this:

1. First, download SoX and add it to your project's classpath. You can do this by running `sox --help` from the command line to print out the location of the SoX executable, and then adding it to your project's build path.

2. Write a method to blend two audio files together. This method would take the paths to both files as arguments, and return a new file containing the blended audio. Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.io.File;
import javax.sound.sampled.*;

public class Blender {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String input1 = "birds.wav";
        String input2 = "children.wav";
        String output = "blend.wav";

        // Load the first audio file
        Clip clip1 = AudioSystem.getClip();
        clip1.open(AudioSystem.getAudioInputStream(new File(input1)));

        // Load the second audio file
        Clip clip2 = AudioSystem.getClip();
        clip2.open(AudioSystem.getAudioInputStream(new File(input2)));

        // Set up the SoX command for blending
        String command = String.format("sox %s -m -0.5%s -t wav -b 16 -e signed -c 1 %s", input1, input2, output);

        // Execute the SoX command
        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
        Process process = runtime.exec(command);
        int exitValue = process.waitFor();

        // Play the blended audio
        Clip finalClip = AudioSystem.getClip();
        finalClip.open(AudioSystem.getAudioInputStream(new File(output)));
        finalClip.setFramePosition(0);
        finalClip.loop(finalClip.LOOP_CONTINUOUSLY);
    }
}
```

