To implement bubble sort for sorting the given array in ascending order with minimal memory usage and error handling, follow these steps:

Step 1: Define a function `bubbleSort` that takes the array and its size as arguments. This function should use two nested loops to iterate through each element of the array, comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. The outer loop runs from the first element to the second-last element, and the inner loop runs from the first element to the current position in the outer loop.

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) {
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

